        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the <strong>power rule for exponents</strong>. </p>",
            "readable_text": "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the power rule for exponents.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To demonstrate, let&#x27;s simplify the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4]</span> by applying the power rule:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4] by applying the power rule:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (3^{5})^{4}|=; 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 3^{5 + 5 + 5 + 5}|=; 3^{5 \u22c5 4}|=; 3^{20}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (3^{5})^{4}|=; 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 3^{5 + 5 + 5 + 5}|=; 3^{5 \u22c5 4}|=; 3^{20}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the power rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4] by applying the power rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (3^{5})^{4}|=; 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 3^{5 + 5 + 5 + 5}|=; 3^{5 \u22c5 4}|=; 3^{20}]"
      },
