    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the power rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4] by applying the power rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (3^{5})^{4}|=; 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5} \u00d7 3^{5}_(\u23df)_(4copies)|=; 3^{5 + 5 + 5 + 5}|=; 3^{5 \u22c5 4}|=; 3^{20}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power Rule With Positive Integer Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(2\\cdot 4^3\\right)^2] as a base raised to a single exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\left(2\\cdot4^3\\right)^2=2^{2}\\cdot 4^{3\\cdot 2}=4 \\cdot 4^{6} = 4^{7}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (0.2^{5})^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 0.2^{7}]\nb. [MATH: 0.2^{10}]\nc. [MATH: 0.4^{5}]\nd. [MATH: 0.2^{3}]\ne. [MATH: 0.4^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3^{4})^{5} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 15^{9}]\nb. [MATH: 15^{20}]\nc. [MATH: 3^{5/4}]\nd. [MATH: 3^{9}]\ne. [MATH: 3^{20}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power Rule With Negative Integer Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^{6}\\right)^{-2}] as a base raised to a single exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^{6}\\right)^{-2}=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^{(6)\\cdot (-2)}=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^{-12}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (6^{\u2212 2})^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6^{\u2212 2}]\nb. [MATH: 6^{2}]\nc. [MATH: 6^{8}]\nd. [MATH: 6^{\u2212 8}]\ne. [MATH: 6^{\u2212 24}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (((1)/(3))^{\u2212 3})^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{\u2212 3}]\nb. [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{4}]\nc. [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{31}]\nd. [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{3}]\ne. [MATH: ((1)/(3))^{\u2212 4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power Rule With Negative Integer Exponents and Formatting the Result\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(2^{-4}\\right)^{2}] as a base raised to a single, positive exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\left(2^{-4}\\right)^{2}=2^{(-4)\\cdot 2}=2^{-8}]\n\nTo evaluate [MATH: 2^{-8},] we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: (2)] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n[MATH: 2^{-8} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8}]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (5^{\u2212 3})^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((1)/(10))^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 5]\nc. [MATH: ((1)/(5))^{6}]\nd. [MATH: 10]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (((1)/(5))^{5})^{\u2212 3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((1)/(5))^{8}]\nb. [MATH: ((1)/(5))^{15}]\nc. [MATH: 5^{8}]\nd. [MATH: 5^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 5^{15}]"
